# Python cache files
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
*$py.class

# Test caches
.pytest_cache/

# Environment variables (if you have .env file)
.env

# Virtual Environment
venv/
# `/env/` is anchored to the repo root ON PURPOSE. An unanchored `env/`
# also matched `deploy/env/`, which holds the deployment TEMPLATES, so
# deploy/10-install-app.sh referenced a file that was never in the
# repository and a fresh clone could not install. A `!deploy/env/*`
# negation cannot fix that: once a DIRECTORY is excluded, git never
# descends into it. The templates carry only <PLACEHOLDER> values; real
# values live in /opt/padyar-*/.env on the host, never here.
/env/

# Database (Local data should usually not be committed)
chat_history.db
chat_history*.db
application_logs.db
application_logs*.db
*.sqlite3

# WAL sidecar files
*.db-wal
*.db-shm

# Automatic DB backups (runtime data)
backups/

# One-time JSON->DB migration snapshot (survives git pull; not source)
migration_snapshot/

# Auto-generated admin credentials (first-run only)
ADMIN_CREDENTIALS.txt

# Provider credential files.
#
# `.env` above is an EXACT name match, so it never covered a file like
# `ASANAK-CREDENTIALS.env` — which sits at the repo root holding the live
# Asanak username, password, API key and SECRET_KEY. It was untracked but not
# ignored, so a single `git add .` would have committed production SMS
# credentials into history.
#
# Deliberately NOT a blanket `*.env`: `.env.example` is tracked on purpose and
# a wildcard would start ignoring a file that is already in the repo. The
# negation below makes that explicit so nobody widens this later by accident.
ASANAK-CREDENTIALS.env
*-CREDENTIALS.env
*_CREDENTIALS.env
*.credentials
!.env.example

# OS generated files
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db

# IDE specific files
.vscode/
.idea/
.venv/

# Claude Code worktrees (transient)
.claude/worktrees/

# Git worktrees for parallel task agents (transient)
.worktrees/

# Superpowers SDD scratch (ledgers, briefs, reports)
.superpowers/

# Large model archives (downloaded, not source)
*.tar.bz2

# Large media files. THE WHOLE media/ TREE, not a list of subdirectories.
#
# media/ is RUNTIME storage, written by the admin panel while the install is in
# use, and a fresh clone is supposed to arrive with it empty. app/main.py
# creates it at startup, so nothing needs it in the repo.
#
# On a server it is a SYMLINK to /var/lib/padyar/<slug>/media (see
# deploy/10-install-app.sh), and deploy/padyar-deploy.sh updates the checkout
# with `git reset --hard`. Those two facts together are why a tracked file under
# media/ is dangerous: reset --hard restores it THROUGH the symlink, straight
# into the customer's live media store, on every single deploy. Five files from
# an old media-upload test were doing exactly that until 2026-08-28.
#
# Listing subdirectories one by one is what let media/uploads/ slip in before
# it was added. Ignore the tree, and add an exception below if a real asset
# ever has to ship.
media/
.gstack/

# Generated speech. TTS_CACHE_DIR points outside the repo on a real install
# (/var/lib/padyar/tts/cache), but a developer running the engine locally gets
# it here, and a cache is regenerable by definition — the panel's "build audio
# for every answer" button is how it comes back. (media/tts-cache/ is already
# covered by the media/ rule above; this one is for the non-media path.)
tts-cache/

# Graphify code-graph output (regenerated, not source)
graphify-out/

# Local embedding model cache (regenerable, ~100MB)
data/models/

# Hugging Face hub cache. app/services/embeddings.py sets HF_HOME to the repo
# cache dir, so a local run of the embedding model drops ~1GB of downloaded
# weights here. Regenerable, never source. tests/test_no_legacy_engine_references.py
# already skips it; this keeps `git add .` from staging it.
.hfcache/

# Knowledge snapshots are runtime state. The freshness checker that wrote
# them is gone, but a working copy from before its removal can still carry
# the directory on disk — keep it out of commits.
content/snapshots/

# OTP dev-only outbox (never in production)
data/otp-dev-outbox.log

# Copies of .env taken before the admin panel rewrites it. `.env` above only
# ignores that exact name, so a timestamped copy would otherwise be committable
# — and it holds the same credentials.
.env.backup.*
.env.*.backup

# Timestamped taxonomy backups written by the admin editor (runtime state).
# data/visit-taxonomy.json itself stays tracked — it is the real taxonomy.
data/visit-taxonomy.backup.*.json

# This install's trained intent classifier: the .npz weights, the JSON
# sidecar that describes them, the version history and the writers' lock
# (app/services/intent.py, ADR-022). A customer's own weights are their data,
# and every file is regenerated by a reindex. Per-install state, never source.
data/intent-model/
# INTENT_MODEL_DIR (app/config.py) can move that directory anywhere, including
# inside the repository, so the rule above is not enough on its own: the file
# names this module writes are ignored wherever they land.
intent-classifier.npz
intent-classifier.json
intent-classifier.history.jsonl
.intent-artifact.lock
.intent-artifact-tmp-*
